    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2881.8,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 789,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The standard form we drink, swim in, and use for weather (rain).\n*   **Solid:** Ice, which forms at or below 0°C (32°F).\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor or steam, which forms when water boils or evaporates.\n\n**Key properties that make water special:**\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry vital minerals and nutrients through our bodies and through the soil.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Unlike most substances, which become denser when they freeze, water actually expands and becomes *less* dense as ice. Because ice floats, lakes and oceans freeze from the top down, allowing marine life to survive underneath the ice during winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps body temperatures stable.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n\nDespite being so abundant, **only about 2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and accessible aquifers).\n\nWater is constantly moving in a continuous cycle called the **water cycle** (or hydrological cycle):\n1.  **Evaporation:** The sun heats water in oceans and lakes, turning it into vapor that rises into the atmosphere.\n2.  **Condensation:** The vapor cools and turns back into liquid droplets, forming clouds.\n3.  **Precipitation:** Water falls back to Earth as rain, snow, sleet, or hail.\n4.  **Collection:** Water flows into rivers, lakes, and oceans, and the cycle starts over.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Importance to Life\nAs far as science knows, **life cannot exist without water.** \n*   **Biological Role:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. It acts as a lubricant for joints, regulates body temperature (through sweat), and flushes out waste. \n*   **Ecosystems:** Water provides habitats for millions of aquatic species and is required by plants to undergo photosynthesis (the process by which plants make their food and release oxygen).\n*   **Human Civilization:** Throughout history, human civilizations have always formed near water sources (rivers and coasts) for drinking, agriculture, hygiene, and transportation.\n\nIn summary, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with extraordinary properties that make life possible."
